  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThis evening, low pressure moves in from the Great Lakes. The low \ncould allow for the potential to see numerous showers overnight. The \nstorm environment looks to be very moisture-lade, with PWATs between \n1.5 and 1.8\". PWATs of this magnitude sit near the 90th percentile, \nand could allow for very efficient rain making showers. However, it \nis not an impressive environment convectively, with poor lapse rates \nand very low CAPE across the region. Some thunder can't be ruled out \nbut mainly expecting heavy showers. CAMs have the most\nconsistent and heaviest swaths of showers across locations \nnorth of the mountains, and has been the trend for the past few \nmodel cycles as well. 70+ kts of effective layer wind shear will\nallow for showers to move fairly progressively over the area, \nbut some showers may also backbuild up north. Flash flooding can\nnot be ruled out with any aforementioned backbuilding showers. \n\nAs mentioned earlier, showers should be more numerous across \nnorthern NH and northwestern ME. 3 hour Flash Flood Guidance \nacross this region is quite low, and is between 1.80-2.50\" of \nrainfall. So it also shouldn't take much rainfall across this \nregion to start noticing some nuisance flooding impacts. \n\nSpeaking of, rainfall amounts look to be fairly hit or miss, with \nlocations north of the mountains likely to see at least a half inch. \nHowever, higher totals locally of 1.5-2.5 inches are possible as \nwell north of the mountains.  Showers are possible south of the \nmountains but they look to be more isolated, as some moisture\ngets channeled to an adjacent low pressure system over the Mid-\nAtlantic. Wetting rains are likely down there.\n\nThe low exits the region Monday morning. The environment could still \nbe fairly moist, with dewpoints in the upper 60s. Instability from \ndaytime heating may allow for the development of a couple isolated \nshowers and storms along the CT River Monday night. In addition,\nfog and low stratus is likely to move onshore and allow for\ngloomy and foggy conditions through the 2nd half of Monday and\ncontinuing into Tuesday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nTuesday, low level moisture may still be entrenched along the \ncoast and interior. It will depend on proximity to a low as it \nexits southern New England and pivots towards/just outside of \nthe Gulf of Maine. Would expect northerly flow behind the low to\nwhittle this away, but just 10 kts at 850mb won't make it \nquick.\n\nGuidance has been initiating some diurnal showers or storms \nTuesday afternoon across portions of the interior where this \nmoisture overlaps enough daytime heating. Model profiles show \nmoisture in the lower levels, but also feature a very dry mid to\nupper level. It is possible warm air aloft also advects in, \ncreating a cap and ultimately limiting a unstable layer. As a \nresult, went with lower precip chances at this range. Not ruling\nout showers and storms, but think they could be more isolated \nin nature given the prominent dry air aloft and ceiling on \ninstability.\n\nWarmer for Wednesday as southerly flow kicks in ahead of an \nelongated cold front. Highs should bump up into the low 80s, a \nfew degrees warmer than Tuesday. While the approaching front \nwill carry precipitation with it, lift is in question as the \nparent surface low is well north, east of Hudson Bay. Thus \nprecip into New England may be dependent on daytime instability \nif timing aligns. \n\nA southern stream disturbance may pass just to the south\nThursday, exiting across the Gulf of Maine or open Atlantic.\nThis could bring NW periphery showers, but we could also see a\ndeep enough dry layer to negate much of this. High pressure then\nlooks to return to the region late week, perhaps in time for a\nsunny start to next weekend.",
